-\emph{Comparisons are paired on the arrival sequence, not on the bin levels.}
-Each sample has one fill sequence. Archived configs point every policy at the
-same dataset file. A freshly generated sample is seeded by the simulation seed
-plus the sample index, which does not depend on the policy name. A second seed,
-which does depend on the policy and the day, resets only that policy's random
-stream and the online predictor of future fill. With the reported sensor noise
-at zero, the policy reads the true post-arrival level. That level is not the
-stored arrival: it is the arrival added to whatever residual the same policy
-left the day before. What is shared is the sequence of arrivals. Kilograms
-still collectable are not, because mass above $E_i$ is lost. Collected tonnage
-is comparable because the arrivals match. The archived dataset file itself is
-not in this tree; its provenance is pending recovery.
+\emph{Comparisons share the increments, not the levels.}
+Given the scenario and the sample, the deposit of bin $i$ on day $d$ is
+fixed by $i$ and $d$. The demand seed does not depend on the policy.
+Archived configurations point every policy in a scenario at the same
+dataset file. A generated sample uses the simulation seed plus the sample
+index only. A second seed, which does depend on the policy and the day,
+resets that policy's random stream and the online predictor, not the
+deposits. What is shared is the sequence $a_{i,d}$. The opening state
+$w_{i,d}$ is not shared: it is the residual that same policy left. With
+sensor noise off, the policy reads the true capped level. Collected
+tonnage is comparable because the arrivals match, not because the
